Explain: This is a simple demonstration of double free vulnerability.
In this code, each queue (rx/tx) will be allocated by alloc_skb(DEF_XXX_Q_ENTRIES)
and deallocated in free_skb().
However, the free_skb() may not actually free the buffer allocated.

In case free_skb() does call skb_free_frags() which frees the buffer at somepoint,
we still can do double free in the following two cases:
1. Calling alloc_netdev(0) with no free_netdev(0), which will do "kzalloc(0)" instead.
Thus we can actually do double free on the skb allocated in line 91.
2. Calling alloc_skb(DEF_XXX_Q_ENTRIES, GFP_DMA, NET_NAME); free_skb(skb);
In this case we will free GFP_DMA memory using normal memory allocated by kzalloc().




