Spectre mitigation mechanism in place - no_sanitize_kernel_nofault(fs, io)
The kernel allows the user to enter arbitrary values in io_sync_cancel's first 
parameter and that could be used as address to retrieve arbitrary data from memory.


```c
static unsigned long __ffs(unsigned long word)
{
        return (__builtin_ffsl(word) - 1);
}

static unsigned long io_fixed_file_slot(struct io_fixed_file_table *f, int idx)
{
	return &f->files[idx];
}

#define FFS_MASK ((1UL << (__FFS_BITS-1)) - 1UL - 1UL)
static unsigned long __ffs(unsigned long word)
{
        return (__builtin_ffsl(word) - 1);
}

static unsigned long io_fixed_file_slot(struct io_fixed_file_table *f, int idx)
{
	return &f->files[idx];
}

#define FFS_MASK ((1UL << (__FFS_BITS-1)) - 1UL - 1UL)
unsigned long io_fixed_file_slot(struct io_fixed_file_table *f, int idx)
{
	return &f->files[idx];
}
```
This function is used to allocate space for user supplied file handles from 
the fixed-size array, in which the user can set the number of file handles with 
the struct io_uring_params.nr_files. This means the user can pass
an arbitrary value for the file handle to check if the allocated space 
from the struct io_uring_params.nr_files. If so, the user would be able to 
retrieve the arbitrary data from the memory because the io_sync_cancel 
function would dereference the struct file pointer, which is the address of 
the arbitrary file handle.

