The above C code block is susceptible to a heap overflow vulnerability, which leads to a remote code execution.

Exploitation involves triggering nft_set_desc_concat_parse() function in nft_set_desc_concat() function.


The reason why we are vulnerable, is because in this code fragment:

	u32 len;

The u32 type is 4 bytes. 

len is local variable that hold a value: ntohl(nla_get_be32(tb[NFTA_SET_FIELD_LEN]))

and tb[NFTA_SET_FIELD_LEN] is a variable that contain a value: nla_get_be32(attr)

The type of attr is struct nlattr *

If we check the definition of the struct nlattr, one can observe that it stores 4 bytes data in attribute:

NLA_HDRLEN being defined as:

define	NLA_HDRLEN	(sizeof(struct nla_hdr))

define 	struct nla_hdr {
	u16	nla_len;
	u16	nla_type;
};

As above code shows, attr contains a pointer to a block of memory which contains 4 bytes data, so the data stored in tb[NFTA_SET_FIELD_LEN] is at beginning of memory block that attr points to. 

nla_get_be32() function then converts this data from network byte order to host byte order , by reading 4 bytes.

If the input data is more than 4 bytes, then this 4 bytes are overwritten

If this was C# application, you should know that when you try to cast 12 bytes to 4 bytes, by casting from long to int, you will lose the last 8 bytes.

