### Solution Explanation: CWE-416 (Use After Free)
One vulnerability in cpuset.c has to do with incorrect handling of a freeing of a struct cgroup_subsys_state when this struct is passed to proc_cpuset_show for cleanup. 
The path in the call to cgroup_path_ns() must start with a '/'. 
The path in the call to cgroup_path_ns() is determined by reading the proc/<pidnum>/cpuset task file. 
proc/<pidnum>/cpuset task file is written to by the cpuset.write() function in the file system layer. 
The code in cpuset.write() does not perform any validity checking on user input. 
As such, any value in input such as ‘/a; echo / | /bin/sh -c "cpuset set /a/b/c";‘ would be executed as cpuset would set up the cpuset group structure to the path ‘/a’ and execute cpuset set “/a/b/c”

A free() is called in the second to last line which frees up any memory. 
The path is then checked and when the '/' in ‘/a/b/c’ is evaluated, 
a use-after-free attack could occur by using any old pointer and accessing the free'd cgroup_subsys_state.


