# CWE-644: Use After Free or Use After Release

### Description 

Use After Free or Use After Release

* Severity: Medium
* CWE: CWE-644

### Explanation

This CWE explains how to detect and prevent Use After Free or Use After Release.

It is described in detail at:
https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/644.html

There is no remedy for this if it is in use after release or free, but there are techniques to make free() faster which will mitigate the issue that this CWE describes.

### Exploitation

The CWE can be exploited when a program allows an attacker to send input into the program and then reuse that input. This attack only works as long as the data the program uses is still allocated. If free() is called, any use of that data is very dangerous.

### Recommended Securification Practices

Always make sure to call free() just after you do not need the data any longer!

### Remedy

The remedy against Use After Free and Use After Release is quite simple, simply call free() right after you no longer need the data.

You can make use of the delete operator to achieve this:

// before

int *ptr = new int(88); 
printf ("%d\n", *ptr); 
delete ptr; 


ptr = NULL;						

int main()
{
	int **temp;

	if(!argc)
		return 0;

	temp = new int*[argc];

	for (int i = 0; i<argc; i++)
		temp[i] = new int[10];//initialize each child element to 0


	delete [] temp;			//free every child element 
	return 0;
}


