Explanation: 
There is a stack buffer overflow issue in the udf_read_inode() function
of fs/udf/inode.c. Since the path parameter passed to udf_read_inode()
is hard coded as a UDF filesystem inode, that may lead to stack buffer
overflow if the path is too long causing a write-what-where attack and
leads to arbitrary overwrite and code execution vulnerability.
Note: The version of the code affected was tested from Linux 3.0 to 4.15
which is around 5 versions.


CWE: CWE-121: Stack Based Buffer Overflow
https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/121.html
https://duo.com/cwshelf/cwe-121

Line 198 and 240 of fs/udf/inode.c contain user input from the disk at a point in which it is loaded onto the stack. 
In addition, line 128 checks that the path length is not larger than PATH_MAX. However, this does not guarantee that the path can be loaded entirely onto the stack. This is because the stack is contiguous and the stack pointer may be incremented by the stack size multiple times if the application allocates multiple variables into the stack or if the number of calls to fs/udf/inode.c are made. Due to the size of the udf_read_inode stack buffers (see line 128 of fs/udf/inode.c), this can lead to a stack overflow.


A stack-based buffer overflow flaw was found in fs/udf/inode.c.

